{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RCA (Phase 1 — confirmed)\n\n**Mechanism (proven).** The replication check has TWO watchers but only one got the designed lag-absorption. `replication_verdict` (src/rebar/mirror_guard.py:44-73) reports instantaneous SHA equality BY DESIGN — its docstring (lines 47-50, shipped verbatim in introducing commit d96628c9dca) says: 'A transient divergence (replication runs ~15s behind a submit) is expected and is NOT treated specially here — the *alarm's* evaluation window (sustained divergence) absorbs transient lag; this function reports the instantaneous truth.' The secondary watcher implements that window: infra/terraform/monitoring_ws7.tf:40-44 requires 2x5-min sustained divergence, with the rationale 'so the normal ~15s post-submit replication lag never pages — only a genuinely stuck mirror does.' The PRIMARY watcher (.github/workflows/mirror-guard.yml) has NO window: it runs `python -m rebar.mirror_guard --replication` once per cron tick and fails on exit 1 — a single instantaneous sample. Sampling inside the expected post-submit replication window (run 32567576681, mid merge-burst) therefore reds the run for self-healing lag; the next tick (32568260546, +16min) passed.\n\n**Runtime confirmation (credential-free, local).** `replication_verdict('8d9acfd2…','d6a2ea0d…')` -> healthy=False, and `run(check_replication=True,…)` with stubbed fetchers returning that diverged pair -> exit code 1 (the workflow's failure condition); the same pair converged -> exit 0. Reproduces the incident pattern exactly. Static confirmation: mirror-guard.yml contains no retry/re-sample/window logic.\n\n**Legitimacy: incomplete-implementation** (unlocks repair per the not-a-bug guard). Cited design authority expecting lag tolerance: (1) the guard's own module docstring (d96628c9dca, src/rebar/mirror_guard.py:48-50) — transient divergence is expected and the ALARM layer must absorb it via a sustained-divergence evaluation window; (2) the sibling watcher's implementation of exactly that window with explicit anti-noise rationale (monitoring_ws7.tf:40-41); (3) the workflow's own stated purpose is to detect that 'replication has stalled' (mirror-guard.yml header), which a single-sample inequality does not establish. The designed absorption was never built on the workflow surface — a designed capability left unwired, not a deliberate strictness choice (no comment or AC anywhere accepts transient-lag red runs on the workflow). Supporting precedent, not controlling: operator ruling on 3ff9-a8f0 ('suppress under normal load — noise, not outage signal') establishes the noise-class policy for expected self-healing conditions; the controlling authority here is the guard's own design text, so no new operator ruling is required to extend 3ff9.\n\n**caused_by attribution: a774-5ca9-3a80-4f6d (sweaty-seal-braid), by scope-of-work.** The single-sample workflow behavior shipped with a774's own introducing commit d96628c9dca alongside the docstring that created the absorption obligation — the plan's AC ('The workflow fails … when either check is unhealthy') baked in single-sample semantics while the same change's design text required sustained-divergence absorption at the alarm layer. No later commit broke this (workflow history: 26d60938d27, 80a4a152beb, d7c5aa0097a, 1384806642f, 7b6e2ad0e31 — token/scope, caching, action bumps, renames only). Note: a774 was FORCE_CLAIMED (plan-review bypassed) and FORCE_CLOSED (verifier bypassed), i.e. the gates that could have caught the omission were bypassed. Close will carry --class=plan_defect --caused-by=a774-5ca9-3a80-4f6d.\n\n**Fix direction (Phase 2, planned next):** in-process, portable persistence in the module CLI path — on a diverged sample, re-sample after a delay and fail only when divergence persists across the window; any non-converged outcome still fails (strictly no weakening: today's failure set minus lag-that-converges-within-window). GitHub-ahead/foreign divergence never converges, so it stays loud. No CI-provider-specific state (satisfies project.portability; the Actions-API previous-run approach was rejected as GitHub-specific)."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787401276727604001,"uuid":"c5179509-4221-46b8-9200-47ce144a44f3"}